    "turn_text": "we're 32 trillion in debt, okay? It's not like there's a big excess pile of cash where we can just continue to give this stuff away. We shouldn't be doing that. There's a responsibility there. They can compensate. They can employ Americans by by buying stuff from us. Now, that's not necessarily the business model you want to grow on, but it is a factor. The other thing is- The paying customer, not a charitable item. That's exactly right. The other thing is to what extent is the American interest? A tremendous amount of the financial transactions, commerce, Transacts through Taiwan and through the US dollar and so there's a direct and immediate financial interest there there's the human interest of course we don't want to see the the communist expansionist model going in so there's another motivation there. But they are willing and able to compensate for equipment. But even then, there's another one. It's because of Ukraine. We have depleted a tremendous amount of our resources by shipping that to Ukraine. And those are reserves typically that would be on hold for American use in case of emergency. But we have sent that out and we've exported that. Quite frankly, we're limited in what we can do on some large scale over the course of time. Of course, you know, that can be built up, but it's any way you slice it, caution is needed before we do anything significant over there.",
